    "name": "Pune MPSC Exam Transparency Protest",
    "location": "Pune city (specific venue not confirmed in reports; protest announced in Pune, likely near MPSC office or a central Pune ground)",
    "cause": "Protesting alleged lack of transparency in Maharashtra Public Service Commission (MPSC) exams, amid a controversy in which a Pune MP was heckled over the issue. MNS chief Raj Thackeray is scheduled to join the protest to back the demand for greater transparency in the exam process.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains high because the protest has continued for multiple days at three sites in Pune despite explicit police denial of permission, prompting Pune Police to issue legal notices to 80 individuals including students and coaching operators. The Bombay High Court has intervened, seeking government details on nearly 70,000 recruitment posts, which signals a tense institutional standoff rather than a resolved situation. The involvement of high-profile political figures such as MNS chief Raj Thackeray, CJP's Abhijeet Dipke, and NCP (SP) leaders is likely to swell crowd sizes and raise the potential for confrontations with police. Although no confirmed injuries or mass detentions have been reported, the unauthorized nature of the gatherings, the denial of permission for the SPPU protest on 17 October, and the government's damage-control measures including sending the MPSC chairman on leave indicate an elevated risk of escalation if enforcement intensifies.",
    "status": "The Pune MPSC Exam Transparency Protest continues as a sustained aspirants' movement in Pune, with thousands of Maharashtra Public Service Commission candidates gathering across the city over alleged paper leaks, evaluation irregularities, and repeated postponements of key exams. Protesters have held satyagraha at three sites in Pune despite Pune Police denying permission, and a CJP protest scheduled at Savitribai Phule University on 17 October was also refused permission by police. Pune Police have initiated legal action, issuing notices to 80 individuals, including students and coaching operators, for continuing the protest without authorization. The Bombay High Court has sought the state government's response on nearly 70,000 MPSC recruitment posts while affirming students' right to protest and urging them to resume classes. Political support has expanded, with MNS chief Raj Thackeray scheduled to join, CJP's Abhijeet Dipke heading to Pune, and NCP (SP) leaders backing the demands. In a significant development, MPSC chairman Vivek Bhimanwar was sent on leave amid the paper leak controversy, and organizers plan to continue the agitation until systemic exam reforms and accountability are delivered.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Pune Police have denied permission for protests at multiple locations, including the CJP protest at Savitribai Phule University on 17 October, and are actively enforcing restrictions at three protest sites in Pune. Legal notices have been issued to 80 individuals. Specific deployment numbers, equipment, and road closures were not confirmed in available reports.", "Recent incidents: Pune Police initiated legal action by issuing notices to 80 individuals, including students and coaching operators, for continuing protests without authorization. The MPSC chairman Vivek Bhimanwar was sent on leave amid the paper leak controversy. No confirmed injuries, arrests, or property damage were reported in the search results.", "Medical access: No specific information on hospitals, ambulances on site, or medical accessibility concerns was available in the search results. No injuries have been reported in connection with the protest.", "Legal support: The Bombay High Court has sought the state government's response on nearly 70,000 MPSC recruitment posts and affirmed students' right to protest while urging them to resume classes. Legal notices have been issued to 80 individuals. CJP's Abhijeet Dipke and NCP (SP) leaders are providing political and organizational support. Specific legal helplines or lawyer involvement were not detailed in the reports."],
