    "location": "Government Tribal Boys' Hostel, Pune (near Pune University Road)",
    "cause": "Tribal students are protesting against the Maharashtra government's age-cap rule for post-matric scholarships and demanding the restoration of the old stipend structure, hostel fee waivers, and immediate disbursal of pending allowances. They also seek a personal hearing with the Tribal Development Minister.",
    "dangerLevel": "moderate",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains high due to the alleged detention of tribal students by police on October 5, 2026, as reported by the Maharashtra Congress, indicating active police intervention against protesters. The planned 180-km march from Nashik to Mumbai, starting September 28, 2026, raises significant risks of confrontations, roadblocks, and logistical hazards along the route. Although the government scrapped the age cap on August 25, 2026, the protest has expanded with new demands including the resignation of Tribal Development Minister Ashok Uike, keeping tensions elevated. The involvement of national figures like Rahul Gandhi and the government's partial concessions have not fully resolved the situation, and the potential for further detentions or clashes remains. No confirmed injuries or mass violence have been reported, but the combination of ongoing protests, police action, and a long march justifies a high danger level.",
    "status": "The Pune tribal students' protest at the Government Tribal Boys' Hostel near Pune University Road continues, centred on a 17-day hunger strike by six students against the Maharashtra government's 30-year age cap for post-matric scholarships and hostel admissions. On August 25, 2026, the government scrapped the age cap and cancelled the August 14, 2026 government resolution, but students expanded their demands to include hostel fee waivers, disbursal of pending allowances, and a personal hearing with Tribal Development Minister Ashok Uike. The protest gained national attention after Rahul Gandhi wrote to Chief Minister Fadnavis and offered to join the students, while a 14-point charter and allegations of invasive practices like menstrual registers and pregnancy tests further fuelled the movement. A planned 180-km march from Nashik to Mumbai was announced for September 28, 2026, demanding Minister Uike's resignation and better facilities in tribal residential schools. On October 5, 2026, Congress alleged that police detained tribal students travelling from Nashik to Mumbai for the protest, indicating ongoing tensions. The government has not fully met the expanded demands, and the situation remains unresolved with potential for further escalation.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Specific deployment numbers, equipment, and road closures at the Government Tribal Boys' Hostel or along the Nashik-Mumbai march route are not detailed in the search results. Reports mention police detention of tribal students travelling from Nashik to Mumbai on October 5, 2026, but no further operational details are available.", "Recent incidents: On October 5, 2026, Congress alleged that police detained tribal students travelling from Nashik to Mumbai for the protest. The government scrapped the 30-year age cap on August 25, 2026, following a 17-day hunger strike by six students. No confirmed injuries or property damage have been reported in the search results.", "Medical access: No information is available in the search results regarding hospitals near the protest site, ambulances on site, or accessibility concerns. The 17-day hunger strike by six students raises potential health risks, but no medical details have been reported.", "Legal support: No specific legal organizations, lawyers, or helplines are mentioned in the search results. The legal status of the detained students and any court interventions remain unclear."],
