    "name": "UP Government School Students Protest Wave Over Teacher Shortage",
    "location": "Multiple government-run residential schools across Lucknow, including campuses near Hazratganj and Aliganj",
    "cause": "Students at government-run residential schools are protesting severe teacher shortages, poor food quality, and inadequate hostel facilities. The protests have spread across five districts in Uttar Pradesh, with students demanding immediate recruitment of teachers and improvement in basic amenities.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains high due to the ongoing nature of the protests and the potential for escalation. Students have resorted to road blockades and hostel lock-ins, disrupting traffic at major intersections in Lucknow, including the Dubagga-Kanpur road. Police have detained several student protesters, and the government's failure to address grievances has led to repeated protests, including one during heavy rain on August 17, 2026. The protest wave has spread to five districts within a month, indicating escalating momentum. While there are no confirmed reports of violence or injuries, the involvement of young students and the government's strict posture, including orders for action on school lapses, raise concerns of confrontation. The situation is tense, and any further police action or student escalation could lead to violence.",
    "status": "Students at government-run residential schools in Lucknow continue their protest wave over severe teacher shortages, poor food quality, and inadequate hostel facilities. The most recent demonstration saw 250 girls from a UP Social Welfare residential school block the Dubagga-Kanpur road, following a similar protest on August 17, 2026, when girls blocked a busy intersection in heavy rain. The protests have spread across five districts in Uttar Pradesh, with students employing road blockades, hostel lock-ins, and chakka jam. In response, Chief Minister Yogi Adityanath has ordered strict action on school lapses and directed senior officials to monitor schools and address teacher shortages. Police have been deployed to contain the protests, and some students have been detained. Students are demanding immediate recruitment of teachers and improvement in basic amenities, and they vow to continue protests until their demands are met. The government has not yet announced a concrete plan to resolve the teacher shortage, and with board exams approaching, tensions remain high.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Police have been deployed to contain the protests, particularly at road blockade sites such as the Dubagga-Kanpur road and busy intersections in Lucknow. Specific numbers and equipment like barricades or water cannons are not detailed in available reports.", "Recent incidents: On August 17, 2026, girls from a government-run residential school blocked a busy intersection in heavy rain, marking the second demonstration in four days. More recently, 250 girls from a UP Social Welfare residential school blocked the Dubagga-Kanpur road. Some students have been detained by police.", "Medical access: No reports of injuries or medical emergencies have been confirmed. Nearby hospitals and ambulance availability are not mentioned in the search results.", "Legal support: No specific legal support organizations or helplines are mentioned. The legal status of detained students is unclear, and no court interventions have been reported."],
