    "name": "Jadavpur University FETSU Clash and Student Protest",
    "location": "Gate 4 of Jadavpur University, Raja Subodh Chandra Mallick Road, Jadavpur, South Kolkata",
    "cause": "Protesting against the forced cancellation of the FETSU (Faculty of Engineering and Technology Students' Union) meeting and alleged police brutality against students, with ABVP and Left-affiliated students demanding the right to hold democratic student union meetings without administrative or police interference.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "Multiple independent reports confirm actual physical violence between rival student groups at Jadavpur University on 19-20 August 2026, not merely tension: ABVP and Left-leaning (SFI/DSF) students clashed during a FETSU general body meeting, with several students from both camps injured and hospitalised at KPC Medical College Hospital. Reports describe stone-pelting, arson, vandalism, torn posters, and flags being set on fire, with clashes spilling onto the streets near Gate 4 where ABVP protesters breached barricades and clashed with police. A complaint naming 11 people was filed, and both blocs accused each other of bringing outsiders onto campus, raising the risk of renewed confrontation. The university has been closed indefinitely and the Vice Chancellor was hospitalised after falling ill during a meeting on the unrest, underscoring the severity. With rival groups planning further demonstrations and a probe committee only now beginning its work, the potential for fresh violence remains high.",
    "status": "Jadavpur University in South Kolkata remains tense after a violent clash between ABVP and Left-leaning (SFI/DSF) student groups that erupted late on 19 August 2026 during a FETSU general body meeting near Gate 4 on Raja Subodh Chandra Mallick Road, continuing past midnight into 20 August. Several students from both camps were injured and admitted to KPC Medical College Hospital, with reports of stone-pelting, arson, vandalism, torn posters, and flags set on fire; both sides accused each other of bringing outsiders onto campus. Fresh clashes spilled onto the streets on 20 August as ABVP protesters breached barricades at Gate 4 and clashed with police, and the BJP publicly distanced itself from the RSS-backed student body. Police were deployed outside the university after authorities sought assistance, and additional security forces remained around the campus amid fears of further clashes. A complaint naming 11 people was filed, and rival student organisations planned demonstrations around the university and the 8B Bus Stand. A probe committee constituted by the university is scheduled to hold its first meeting this week after the Vice Chancellor, who had fallen ill during a meeting on the unrest, rejoined. No negotiated settlement or formal conclusion of the FETSU dispute has been announced.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Police were deployed outside Jadavpur University after authorities sought assistance; additional security forces remained around the campus. ABVP protesters breached barricades at Gate 4 on 20 August and clashed with police. Specific numbers of personnel and equipment like water cannons or drones are not detailed in available reports.", "Recent incidents: On 19-20 August 2026, ABVP and Left-leaning (SFI/DSF) students clashed during a FETSU general body meeting near Gate 4, with stone-pelting, arson, vandalism, torn posters, and flags set on fire. Several students from both camps were injured and admitted to KPC Medical College Hospital. Fresh clashes occurred on 20 August as ABVP protesters breached barricades and clashed with police. A complaint naming 11 people was filed.", "Medical access: Injured students from both camps were admitted to KPC Medical College Hospital, which is near the university. No reports of ambulances being blocked or medical access impeded. The Vice Chancellor was also hospitalised after falling ill during a meeting on the unrest.", "Legal support: A complaint naming 11 people has been filed in connection with the clash. A university probe committee is scheduled to hold its first meeting this week after the Vice Chancellor rejoined. No specific legal aid organisations or helplines for detained protesters are mentioned in the available reports."],
