    "name": "DMK Thanjavur Cauvery Water Rights Protest",
    "location": "Thanjavur district (Delta region), with solidarity demonstrations expected in Chennai at DMK headquarters, Anna Salai",
    "cause": "DMK is protesting against what it calls TVK's betrayal of farmers and demanding firm steps to protect Tamil Nadu's Cauvery water rights, along with a special relief package for delta farmers affected by water shortages.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "The situation escalated beyond the prior assessment: Udhayanidhi Stalin was arrested by a joint Thanjavur-Neelankarai police team despite a Madras High Court oral order that no action be taken until a noon hearing, and he was taken on a roughly 300-km transit for questioning. DMK workers protested the detention across parts of Tamil Nadu, including Chennai, and a Pudukkottai MLA was among those detained, indicating multiple arrests and a tense standoff between police and party cadres. There are no confirmed reports of lathi charges, tear gas, or protester injuries, so the level is high rather than critical, but the arrest of a senior opposition leader and the resulting statewide protests carry clear potential for further escalation. The controversy over Udhayanidhi's remark has added a volatile political dimension, drawing sharp criticism online and from rival parties, which could inflame tensions further.",
    "status": "The DMK staged its announced Cauvery water rights demonstration in Thanjavur on August 3, 2026, with Leader of the Opposition Udhayanidhi Stalin leading the protest and criticising the TVK-led government as 'incompetent' over the Cauvery issue and farmers' plight. The demonstration demanded firm steps to protect Tamil Nadu's Cauvery water rights and a special relief package for delta farmers, and coincided with Chief Minister Vijay's proposed Karnataka visit over the Mekedatu dam row. During the event, Udhayanidhi made a remark after supporters chanted a film actress's name, triggering a political and online controversy; DMK spokesperson TKS Elangovan later defended him, saying people were twisting his speech. Udhayanidhi was subsequently detained for questioning by a joint team of Thanjavur and Neelankarai police, and reports state the Madras High Court had orally ordered no action until a noon hearing, yet police arrested him by 11 a.m. and took him on a roughly 300-km transit for probe. Protests erupted in parts of Tamil Nadu, including Chennai, after the detention, with a Pudukkottai MLA among those detained; the DMK alleged the arrest was aimed at preventing him from raising the Cauvery issue in the Legislative Assembly. DMK chief M.K. Stalin has separately urged Tamil Nadu Chief Minister Vijay to convene an all-party meeting on the Cauvery dispute.",
    "details": ["Police presence: A joint team of Thanjavur and Neelankarai police detained Udhayanidhi Stalin in Chennai; he was taken on a roughly 300-km transit for questioning. Protests erupted in parts of Tamil Nadu, including Chennai, after the detention. Specific numbers of police deployed, equipment like barricades or water cannons, and road closures are not detailed in the search results.", "Recent incidents: On August 3, 2026, Udhayanidhi Stalin led a DMK demonstration in Thanjavur over Cauvery water rights and farmers' issues. During the event, he made a remark after supporters chanted a film actress's name, sparking controversy. He was subsequently detained by police, and a Pudukkottai MLA was among those detained during protests against his arrest. No injuries or property damage are reported.", "Medical access: No information available in the search results regarding hospitals nearby, ambulances on site, or injuries reported.", "Legal support: The Madras High Court had orally ordered no action against Udhayanidhi until a noon hearing, but police arrested him by 11 a.m. DMK spokesperson TKS Elangovan defended Udhayanidhi's remark. No specific legal aid organizations or helplines are mentioned."],
