    "location": "Outside Cockroach Janta Party founder's residence/office, Ahmedabad",
    "cause": "Two youths from Ahmedabad are demanding the resignation of India's Chief Election Commissioner over alleged irregularities in the voter list. They are protesting under the banner of the Cockroach Janta Party and have initiated an indefinite hunger strike.",
    "dangerLevel": "critical",
    "dangerReason": "The situation remains critical due to mass detentions and heavy police crackdown. Over 70 protesters were detained in Ahmedabad, including women, journalists, and seniors, and activists allege further detentions. In Delhi, chaos erupted as protesters tried to reach Jantar Mantar, and high-profile leaders like Neha Bora were detained. The active enforcement of restrictions and widespread detentions indicate a severe crackdown with a high risk of further escalation and potential violence. Although a court released some leaders on October 11, 2026, the protest continues and police posture remains aggressive.",
    "status": "The Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) hunger strike in Ahmedabad, led by two youths demanding the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar over alleged voter list irregularities, remains ongoing outside the CJP founder's residence/office. Ahmedabad police have detained over 70 protesters, including women, journalists, and senior citizens, in connection with the protest, and activists allege further detentions. The movement has spread nationwide: hundreds protested in Mumbai, with celebrities like Shabana Azmi, Vishal Bhardwaj, and Vishal Dadlani joining at Shivaji Park; in Delhi, chaos erupted as protesters tried to reach Jantar Mantar, and AISA president Neha Bora was detained near INA market. On October 11, 2026, a court released the CJP founder and two other leaders from detention. Opposition lawmakers disrupted parliament on Wednesday in support of the protesters. The indefinite hunger strike continues, and no resolution has been reached.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Ahmedabad police detained 70+ protesters, including women, journalists, and seniors. In Delhi, police locked down the capital with barricades to prevent marches toward Jantar Mantar, and chaos erupted as protesters tried to reach the site. Specific numbers of deployed police not provided.", "Recent incidents: On October 11, 2026, a court released the CJP founder and two other leaders from detention. Over 70 were detained in Ahmedabad. In Delhi, AISA president Neha Bora was detained near INA market. Opposition lawmakers disrupted parliament on Wednesday. No injuries reported in search results.", "Medical access: No specific information on medical access or injuries available in search results. Sonam Wangchuk's hunger strike is mentioned separately, but no link to this protest's medical situation.", "Legal support: A court released the CJP founder and two other leaders on October 11, 2026, according to their lawyer. No specific legal aid organizations or helplines mentioned. Detained protesters' legal status unclear beyond the released leaders."],
