    "location": "Chandigarh Police Lines / Sector 17 area, Chandigarh",
    "cause": "Protesters are demanding the immediate release of Sikh prisoners who have completed their sentences, as well as parole for prisoners convicted in the former Punjab Chief Minister Beant Singh assassination case. The demonstration escalated into a direct confrontation with Chandigarh Police.",
    "dangerLevel": "critical",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains critical because Chandigarh Police have escalated to deploying tear-gas shells from drones and using water cannons against protesters at the Chandigarh-Mohali border, representing a serious increase in the use of force. Protesters attempted to breach police barricades, leading to direct clashes and a tense standoff that required chemical irritants to disperse crowds. The situation has expanded beyond Chandigarh with a statewide Punjab bandh on August 21 that shut down transport, markets, and schools, indicating widespread mobilization and disruption. Additionally, an FIR has been filed against MLA Manpreet Singh Ayali and 15 other leaders over a July 29 protest involving 2,500-3,000 people, showing authorities are pursuing legal action against organizers. The use of drone-deployed tear gas represents a new and dangerous tactical escalation, and with further marches planned, the potential for renewed violence remains high.",
    "status": "The Qaumi Insaaf Morcha agitation in Chandigarh remains active and volatile, with the most recent confirmed escalation occurring when protesters attempted to breach police barricades at the Chandigarh-Mohali border while marching toward Punjab Lok Bhawan. Chandigarh Police responded by dropping tear-gas shells from drones and deploying water cannons against the crowd, marking a significant tactical escalation in the use of force. The protesters, organized under the Qaumi Insaaf Morcha banner, are demanding the release of Bandi Singhs (Sikh prisoners who have completed their sentences) and parole for prisoners convicted in the Beant Singh assassination case. A coalition of Sikh religious and political organizations subsequently called for a statewide Punjab bandh on August 21, which crippled transport, shut markets and commercial establishments, and forced private schools to declare holidays while Panjab University rescheduled exams. Separately, Chandigarh Police lodged an FIR against MLA Manpreet Singh Ayali and 15 other leaders of Akali Dal (Waris Punjab De) over a July 29 protest near Gaushala Chowk where nearly 2,500 to 3,000 supporters had gathered. Former Punjab CM Beant Singh's assassin, Hawara, issued a video message from prison urging participants to follow leaders' directions and maintain peace, while organizers have vowed to continue the agitation until their demands are met.",
