    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains high due to the heavy security deployment across Jammu and Kashmir, including police, Army, and CRPF personnel, ahead of the August 5 protests. Authorities imposed restrictions that prevented large-scale gatherings, and the J&K Police warned of legal action against unauthorised protests, indicating a tense standoff. The simultaneous BJP celebrations and opposition protests created a volatile political environment, with historical precedents of clashes during such anniversaries. While no specific incidents of violence or mass detentions were reported in the current intelligence, the combination of restrictions, heavy security, and the ruling party leading protests against the administration it controls elevates the risk of escalation. The situation remains fluid, and any violation of restrictions could trigger police action, potentially leading to detentions or clashes.",
    "status": "The National Conference (NC) held a protest at its Srinagar headquarters on August 5, 2026, marking the seventh anniversary of the abrogation of Article 370 as a 'Black Day'. The PDP and Congress held separate protests in Jammu and other areas, while the BJP organised celebratory functions and a bike rally in Srinagar and Jammu. Security was tightened across Jammu and Kashmir with heavy deployment of police, Army, and CRPF personnel, and authorities imposed restrictions that curtailed large-scale gatherings. The J&K Police issued an advisory warning of legal action against protests, marches, or rallies held without prior permission. Senior NC leaders, including Chief Minister Omar Abdullah, participated in the protests. The NC has announced plans to continue its political campaign for the restoration of Article 370 and full statehood through democratic means. No incidents of violence or mass detentions were reported in the available search results.",
