    "location": "Selakui Industrial Area, Dehradun",
    "cause": "Workers are protesting against the factory management's failure to implement minimum wage revisions and delayed salary payments, which has led to months of unpaid wages for over 200 laborers.",
    "dangerLevel": "critical",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains critical because the protest has already produced confirmed violence: stone-pelting and vandalism at the Selaqui industrial estate on 16 May, followed by police action including lathi charges and tear gas that reportedly injured workers. Police have booked over 500 people and arrested at least 10 to 13 individuals, a mass legal action that signals an active crackdown rather than de-escalation. Prohibitory orders under Section 163 BNSS have been imposed across the Selaqui and SIDCUL industrial areas, restricting assembly and movement, which raises the risk of confrontation if workers attempt further gatherings. The workers' union has refused to attend conciliation until arrested members are released, and workers have threatened to block the Dehradun-Saharanpur highway or set fire to the factory, keeping the potential for renewed clashes high. Police suspicion of links to the Noida unrest further raises the prospect of continued detentions and a hardened security posture.",
    "status": "The labour unrest at the Selaqui (Selakui) industrial estate in Dehradun remains unresolved following a violent escalation on 16 May, when a workers' protest over unpaid wages and overtime turned violent with stone-pelting and vandalism reported at the estate. Workers had launched protests on the evening of 15 May outside factories including Lightenium Technology Pvt Ltd, Dixon Technology and Global Medicoz, demanding wage hikes and payment of months of arrears owed to over 200 labourers. On 16 May, the agitation spiralled into violence as workers came out onto the roads and blocked the highway, and police booked 500-600 unidentified people in connection with the violence, with at least 10 to 13 people arrested. SSP Dehradun Pramendra Dobhal characterised the violence as the work of 'anti-social elements' pelting stones under the guise of a labour protest, and police are investigating suspected links to the Noida labour unrest, including probing WhatsApp groups. The district administration, on the directions of District Magistrate Savin Bansal, imposed prohibitory orders under Section 163 BNSS across the Selaqui and SIDCUL industrial areas to maintain law and order. The factory remains shut, and workers have threatened to block the Dehradun-Saharanpur highway if wage arrears are not cleared, while the workers' union has refused conciliation talks until arrested members are released. No confirmed reports of fresh violence or a formal end to the protest have emerged since the initial crackdown.",
