    "name": "CJP Public Gathering at Azad Maidan",
    "location": "Azad Maidan, Panaji, Goa",
    "cause": "Organized by the Citizens for Justice and Peace (CJP), led by chief Dipke, this public gathering is part of a broader protest movement. The CJP had initially planned to commence a protest march from Goa, but the plan was altered to hold a static gathering at Azad Maidan.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level is high due to active police crackdown and multiple detentions during the protest. On October 11, 2026, heavy police deployment and barricades blocked Azad Maidan, forcing protesters to shift to Panjim Municipal Garden, where several activists were detained. The Bombay High Court ordered the release of six to seven detainees, citing no BNSS Section 163 restrictions, indicating that detentions were legally questionable. An FIR has been filed against approximately 1,000 unidentified persons for an earlier unlawful assembly at Azad Maidan, and recent solidarity marches led to detentions, including that of CJP supporter Mohammad Danish. The North Goa District Magistrate denied permission for the gathering, and police action included thwarting the protest, creating a volatile environment where further detentions or clashes are plausible.",
    "status": "The CJP public gathering at Azad Maidan in Panaji, Goa, on Sunday, October 11, 2026, was blocked by heavy police deployment and barricades after the North Goa District Magistrate denied permission, citing Umar Khalid support as a reason. Organizers shifted the protest to the Panjim Municipal Garden, where several demonstrators were detained as they challenged the denial. The Bombay High Court intervened in a special late-night Sunday hearing, ordering the immediate release of six to seven activists, including hotelier Jack Sukhija, Amreen Shaikh, Aditya Parulekar, and Bhupesh Bin, noting that no BNSS Section 163 restrictions were in place. The protest, backed by the 'Enough is Enough' movement and Justice Ferdino Rebello, seeks the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar. Police had earlier filed an FIR against around 1,000 unidentified persons for an unlawful assembly at Azad Maidan, and recent solidarity marches led to detentions, including CJP supporter Mohammad Danish. The situation remains tense, with legal challenges ongoing and the government maintaining a restrictive posture.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Heavy police deployment and barricades at Azad Maidan on October 11, 2026, blocked entry. Police cited BNSS provisions and past violations to deny permission. No specific numbers or equipment like water cannons or drones were reported.", "Recent incidents: On October 11, 2026, police detained seven CJP activists at Panjim Municipal Garden after they shifted from Azad Maidan. The Bombay High Court ordered their immediate release, noting no BNSS Section 163 restrictions were in place. An FIR was filed against ~1,000 unidentified persons for an earlier unlawful assembly at Azad Maidan.", "Medical access: No information available on hospitals, ambulances, or injuries from the search results.", "Legal support: The Bombay High Court intervened, ordering release of detained activists. Organizations like 'Enough is Enough' and Justice Ferdino Rebello supported the protest. No helplines or specific legal aid groups were mentioned."],
