    "location": "Statewide, all educational institutions across Imphal and Manipur",
    "cause": "The Manipur government ordered all educational institutes closed for three days from August 20, 2026, in response to escalating protests demanding NRC updation before the Census. The shutdown is a direct government response to the twin strike and widespread agitation disrupting normal life.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "The situation remains high risk due to the statewide shutdown of all educational institutions from August 20 to 23, 2026, imposed under the 'prevailing law and order situation,' reflecting the government's recognition of serious unrest. Protests have escalated with road blockades, effigy burnings, and rallies across Imphal and valley districts, and a two-day strike called by students. The unrest has spread to Naga villages, where four villages were attacked and a pastor injured, indicating a communal dimension and potential for further violence. Although no mass casualties have been confirmed in the latest reports, the involvement of multiple groups and the government's precautionary closure suggest a tense environment with potential for escalation. The Chief Minister's meeting with the Union Home Minister underscores the gravity of the situation.",
    "status": "The Manipur government ordered all educational institutions statewide closed for three days from August 20 to 23, 2026, citing the 'prevailing law and order situation' amid escalating protests demanding NRC updation before the Census. The Imphal Valley wore a deserted look as shops, markets, and commercial establishments shuttered alongside schools and colleges, and public transport stayed shut in five valley districts. Students held protests and called a two-day strike, while the JFD group announced a boycott of government offices. Four Naga villages were attacked and a pastor injured after the NRC shutdown hit Imphal Valley, indicating the unrest has spread beyond the valley. Chief Minister N. Biren Singh met Union Home Minister Amit Shah in Delhi to discuss the situation. Authorities have not indicated whether the closure will extend beyond August 23.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Not specified in available reports, but given the statewide shutdown and unrest, police are likely deployed across Imphal and valley districts to enforce restrictions and maintain order.", "Recent incidents: Four Naga villages were attacked and a pastor injured after the NRC shutdown hit Imphal Valley; protests included effigy burnings, road blockades, and rallies across Imphal and other valley districts; a two-day strike was called by students.", "Medical access: No specific information available on medical access or injuries beyond the pastor injured in Naga village attacks; hospitals and ambulances not mentioned in reports.", "Legal support: No information available on legal support organizations, lawyers, or court interventions for detained protesters; the JFD group announced a boycott of government offices, but no legal helplines or status of detainees reported."],
