    "dangerReason": "The situation remains high-risk due to the Meghalaya High Court issuing contempt notices to bar association office-bearers on October 8, 2026, which could lead to legal repercussions and further escalation. The October 7 protest saw over 1,000 lawyers marching through Shillong, indicating large-scale mobilization and potential for confrontations. A threatening letter containing two live bullets was sent to two advocates approximately four days before the previous assessment, representing a direct physical threat. The combination of mass protests, legal action against organizers, and a credible threat of violence sustains a volatile environment. The government's refusal to engage, citing sub judice, and the AG's refusal to resign add to the tension.",
    "status": "The Meghalaya High Court Bar Association and Shillong Bar Association continue their agitation demanding the removal of Advocate General Amit Kumar, with the most recent major action being a protest march in Shillong on October 7, 2026, in which over 1,000 advocates participated. This followed an earlier large-scale rally on August 27, 2026, described as one of the largest protests by the state's legal fraternity. The lawyers have also engaged in a statewide court boycott and work abstention, with bail and urgent matters exempted. The Meghalaya High Court has issued contempt notices to bar association office-bearers over the boycott, terming the strike 'completely illegal.' Chief Minister Conrad Sangma cited sub judice to deny information in the Assembly regarding the protests, and the Advocate General has refused to step down. The standoff remains unresolved, with no indication of the protest concluding.",
