    "name": "Bandi Sikh March for Release of Sikh Prisoners (SGPC-Backed)",
    "location": "Sector 17 to Punjab Raj Bhavan, Chandigarh; assembly points at Sector 17 Plaza and Gurdwara Amb Sahib, Mohali",
    "cause": "Demanding the immediate release of Sikh prisoners who have completed their sentences, including those convicted in the Beant Singh assassination case, and seeking parole for jailed Sikh activists. The march is backed by the Shiromani Gurdwara Parbandhak Committee (SGPC) and follows the surfacing of a video by a jailed extremist ahead of the agitation.",
    "dangerLevel": "high",
    "dangerReason": "The danger level remains high because the movement has already produced confirmed state force against protesters: on August 15, 2026, Chandigarh police deployed tear gas and water cannons against demonstrators attempting to breach barricades near the Punjab Raj Bhavan, resulting in detentions and injuries. The agitation then escalated into a statewide Punjab Bandh on August 21, 2026, which crippled transport and shut markets, schools, colleges and banks, demonstrating the organisers' capacity to disrupt normal life across the state. On the same day, police physically stopped a march towards Punjab Lok Bhawan at the Chandigarh-Mohali border, creating a tense confrontation that could have deteriorated further. The involvement of the SGPC and Qaumi Insaaf Morcha has mobilised thousands, and the surfacing of a video by jailed extremist Hawara has heightened security concerns about radical elements joining the march. While no mass violence has been reported during the bandh itself, the combination of a 3.5-year-old siege at the Mohali border, repeated police-protestor confrontations, and the stated intention to continue until demands are met keeps the potential for escalation high.",
    "status": "The Bandi Sikh March, backed by the Shiromani Gurdwara Parbandhak Committee (SGPC) and the Qaumi Insaaf Morcha, remains an active and highly mobilised movement centred on Chandigarh and the Chandigarh-Mohali border. The Qaumi Insaaf Morcha has maintained a sit-in at the Chandigarh-Mohali border since January 7, 2023, and the agitation escalated sharply on August 15, 2026, when Chandigarh police used tear gas and water cannons against protesters attempting to breach barricades near the Punjab Raj Bhavan, resulting in detentions and injuries. The SGPC subsequently extended its backing to the march, adding significant organisational weight. On August 21, 2026, the Qaumi Insaaf Morcha's Punjab Bandh call shut shops, markets, commercial establishments and private schools across the state, with traffic severely hit on highways; on the same day, protesters marching towards Punjab Lok Bhawan were stopped by police at the Chandigarh-Mohali border, creating a tense standoff. Ahead of the march, a video message from jailed extremist Hawara surfaced in which he urged participants to follow their leaders' directions and maintain peace, even as security agencies flagged concerns about radical infiltration. Separately, Damdami Taksal announced a protest outside the SGPC headquarters in Amritsar, indicating widening mobilisation within Sikh political and religious institutions. Organisers have vowed to continue until Sikh prisoners who have completed their sentences, including those convicted in the Beant Singh assassination case, are released and parole is granted to jailed activist Hawara. No major fresh clashes have been reported since the August 21 bandh, but the situation remains tense with the potential for further escalation.",
    "details": ["Police presence: Chandigarh police deployed tear gas and water cannons on August 15, 2026, against protesters attempting to breach barricades near the Punjab Raj Bhavan. On August 21, 2026, police stopped a march towards Punjab Lok Bhawan at the Chandigarh-Mohali border. Specific troop numbers and equipment details beyond these incidents are not available in the search results.", "Recent incidents: On August 15, 2026, police used tear gas and water cannons on protesters near Punjab Raj Bhavan, causing injuries and detentions. On August 21, 2026, the Qaumi Insaaf Morcha's Punjab Bandh shut markets, schools and transport across Punjab, and a march towards Punjab Lok Bhawan was stopped at the Chandigarh-Mohali border, creating tension. No further major incidents have been reported since.", "Medical access: Specific information on hospitals, ambulances on site, or medical access during the protests is not available in the search results. Injuries were reported from the August 15, 2026 tear gas and water cannon incident, but no details on medical treatment or accessibility were provided.", "Legal support: Specific information on legal organisations, lawyers, helplines, or court interventions related to detained protesters is not available in the search results. Detentions occurred during the August 15, 2026 incident, but no details on legal status or support were provided."],
